---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'start', label: 'Starting the server' },
   { id: 'tools', label: 'Tools' },
   { id: 'wcag', label: 'wcag_show, wcag_criteria, wcag_search, wcag_rule' },
   { id: 'axe', label: 'run_axe, tree, audit' },
   { id: 'sr', label: 'sr_session and sr_action' },
   { id: 'sr-checks', label: 'sr_list, sr_expect, sr_transcript' },
   { id: 'resources', label: 'Resources' },
   { id: 'effects', label: 'Side effects' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="MCP reference"
   summary="Tool names, inputs, resource URIs, and side effects for the a11ied MCP server. Every tool calls the same runtime function as the matching CLI command, so results match across the two, and every tool name generated from the CLI command registry keeps them from drifting apart."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="split panel" id="start">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Startup</p>
            <h2>Starting the server</h2>
         </div>
         <pre><code>npx -y a11ied mcp</code></pre>
         <p>
            Configure your client to run that command over stdio. The server opens no network port
            and takes no options.
         </p>
         <pre data-label="CONFIG"><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "a11ied": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "a11ied", "mcp"]
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Defaults</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            Every tool that takes <code>version</code> defaults to <code>2.2</code>. The other
            supported value is <code>2.1</code>.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger" id="tools">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Tools</p>
         <h2>The 13 tools</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>doctor</code><span>Return runtime details and the target support matrix. Same as <code>a1 doctor</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag_show</code><span>Return one criterion with its techniques, failures, coverage, and strategy. Same as <code>a1 wcag show</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag_criteria</code><span>List criteria, filtered by level or summarized. Same as <code>a1 wcag criteria</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag_search</code><span>Return ranked criterion matches for a query. Same as <code>a1 wcag search</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag_rule</code><span>Map an axe-core rule id to its criteria and fix guidance. Same as <code>a1 wcag rule</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>run_axe</code><span>Run axe-core against a target. Same as <code>a1 axe</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>tree</code><span>Print the accessibility tree for a target. Same as <code>a1 tree</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>audit</code><span>Run axe, the tree summary, and applicability together. Same as <code>a1 audit</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr_session</code><span>Start, open, stop, or check the one active screen reader session. Same as <code>a1 sr start</code>, <code>open</code>, <code>stop</code>, <code>status</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr_action</code><span>Run one action against the active session. Covers every other <code>a1 sr</code> subcommand except <code>expect</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>batch</code>, and <code>walk</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr_list</code><span>List the named commands <code>sr_action</code>'s <code>perform</code> action runs. Same as <code>a1 sr list</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr_expect</code><span>Check that the session announced a phrase. Same as <code>a1 sr expect</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr_transcript</code><span>Print the session transcript, or write it to a file. Same as <code>a1 sr transcript</code>.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
         <code>a1 sr batch</code> and <code>a1 sr walk</code> have no matching tool. <code>batch</code>
         reads a file of actions for shell scripting. Call <code>sr_action</code> once per step
         instead. <code>walk</code> composes <code>sr_session</code> (action <code>start</code>) with
         <code>sr_action</code> (action <code>read-all</code>) and <code>sr_transcript</code>. Call
         those three tools in that order.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="wcag">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Standards</p>
         <h2>wcag_show, wcag_criteria, wcag_search, wcag_rule</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag_show</code><span><code>criterion</code> required, an id such as <code>1.4.3</code> or a slug such as <code>contrast-minimum</code>. Always returns coverage and strategy alongside the criterion.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag_criteria</code><span><code>level</code> optional: <code>A</code>, <code>AA</code>, or <code>AAA</code>. Omit it to list every criterion. Set <code>summary: true</code> for coverage totals per level instead of the list.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag_search</code><span><code>query</code> required. <code>limit</code> defaults to 10 and caps at 50.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag_rule</code><span><code>ruleId</code> required, an axe-core rule id such as <code>color-contrast</code>. Returns the criteria it covers and axe's help text and help URL.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
         All four read bundled data. They make no network request and start no browser, so they are
         safe for an agent to call freely.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="axe">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Page checks</p>
         <h2>run_axe, tree, audit</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>target</code><span>An http(s) URL or a local file path.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>html</code><span>Inline HTML markup to load instead of <code>target</code>.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
         All three take one page target, exactly like the CLI's positional <code>&lt;target&gt;</code>
         and <code>--html</code>. <code>run_axe</code> also takes <code>criterion</code>,
         <code>level</code>, or <code>ruleIds</code> to narrow the run, and rejects more than one of
         them. Omit all three, and <code>run_axe</code> runs every mapped rule, matching
         <code>a1 axe</code> with no filter. <code>tree</code> takes <code>role</code> and
         <code>name</code> to keep only matching nodes and their ancestors.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>run_axe</code> and <code>audit</code> each return a <code>verdict</code> object and an
         <code>exitCode</code>. <code>exitCode</code> is 4, the CLI's assertion exit code, when a
         violation at or above <code>failOn</code> (default <code>minor</code>) is not covered by
         <code>baseline</code>, and 0 otherwise. Pass <code>baseline</code> as
         <code>{`{ acceptedFindings: [...] }`}</code> to accept known findings, the same list the CLI
         reads from a <code>--baseline</code> file.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="sr">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Screen reader</p>
         <h2>sr_session and sr_action</h2>
      </div>
      <h3>sr_session</h3>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>action</code><span><code>start</code>, <code>open</code>, <code>stop</code>, or <code>status</code>. Required.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>target</code><span>(<code>start</code> only) <code>voiceover</code>, <code>nvda</code>, or <code>virtual</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>allowVirtual</code><span>(<code>start</code> only) Set to <code>true</code> to accept the simulated reader when a real one is available.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>url</code><span>(<code>start</code> and <code>open</code>) The page to load. Virtual loads it directly. VoiceOver and NVDA open it in a system browser and refocus it.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>app</code><span>(<code>start</code> only) A native app that is already open, to read instead of a page.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
         One session is active at a time, so no action takes a session id, and <code>start</code>
         stops any session already active. The start response carries <code>session</code>, which
         includes <code>sessionId</code>, <code>target</code>, and <code>targetType</code>.
         <code>targetType</code> is <code>real</code> for VoiceOver and NVDA, and
         <code>simulated</code> for virtual. <code>status</code> returns
         <code>{`{ noSession: true }`}</code> instead of throwing when no session is active.
      </p>
      <h3>sr_action</h3>
      <p>
         Takes the same <code>action</code> and <code>payload</code> shapes the CLI's driver runtime
         takes, one action per call. The portable verbs need no payload and work on every target:
         <code>next</code>, <code>previous</code>, <code>interact</code>, <code>stop-interacting</code>,
         <code>activate</code>, <code>top</code>, <code>bottom</code>, <code>escape</code>,
         <code>read</code>, <code>transcript</code>, and <code>title</code>. <code>next</code> and
         <code>previous</code> take an optional payload to jump by kind: heading, link, landmark,
         control, button, table, list, graphic, region, or form-field.
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>press</code><span>Payload <code>{`{ keys }`}</code>, one chord per entry, pressed in order, such as <code>["Tab", "Tab"]</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>type</code><span>Payload <code>{`{ text }`}</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>checkpoint</code><span>Payload <code>{`{ label }`}</code>, a named point in the transcript for <code>sr_expect</code>'s <code>since</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>perform</code><span>Payload <code>{`{ command, commandSet? }`}</code>. Runs a named screen reader command. Call <code>sr_list</code> for the names.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>focus</code><span>Payload with one of <code>appName</code>, <code>bundleId</code>, <code>processName</code>, <code>pid</code>, or <code>windowTitle</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>find</code><span>Payload <code>{`{ text }`}</code>. Moves to the next place the text appears.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>table</code><span>Payload <code>{`{ move }`}</code>: <code>next-cell</code>, <code>row-header</code>, and the other table moves.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>elements</code><span>Payload <code>{`{ kind, max? }`}</code>. The rotor for one kind, from the top of the page.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>read-all</code><span>Payload <code>{`{ max? }`}</code>. Say-all as a transcript from the cursor.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>goto</code><span>Payload <code>{`{ role?, name?, max? }`}</code>. Steps forward until the item matches.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wait</code><span>Payload <code>{`{ for?, ms?, timeoutMs? }`}</code>. Polls the transcript for a phrase.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>screenshot</code><span>Payload <code>{`{ path }`}</code>. VoiceOver cursor only.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
         <code>find</code>, <code>goto</code>, and <code>wait</code> add <code>exitCode</code> to the
         result: 4 when the search or wait did not match, matching <code>a1 sr find</code>,
         <code>goto</code>, and <code>wait</code>, and 0 otherwise. Every response's
         <code>state</code> carries the timestamped <code>transcript</code> so far, and a VoiceOver
         session's state also carries <code>axFocusedElement</code>: the accessibility role, subrole,
         title, description, value, and enabled state of the element system keyboard focus is on.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="sr-checks">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Checks</p>
            <h2>sr_list, sr_expect, sr_transcript</h2>
         </div>
         <div class="route-list">
            <div class="route-row"><code>sr_list</code><span><code>query</code>, <code>sr</code>, and <code>commandSet</code> all optional. Start with <code>query</code>. The unfiltered list is over 400 entries.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>sr_expect</code><span><code>pattern</code> required, text (case-insensitive) or <code>/regex/flags</code>. <code>since</code> limits the check to phrases after a checkpoint. <code>not</code> passes when the phrase was NOT announced.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>sr_transcript</code><span><code>since</code>, <code>tail</code>, <code>out</code>, and <code>format</code> all optional. <code>out</code> also writes the transcript to a <code>.json</code> or <code>.md</code> path.</span></div>
         </div>
         <p>
            <code>sr_expect</code> returns <code>exitCode</code> 4 (the CLI assertion exit code) and a
            <code>message</code> when the phrase did not match, and 0 when it did.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Assertion pattern</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            Move the session with <code>sr_action</code>, then call <code>sr_expect</code> to check
            what was announced. This is the same pattern the agent skill's screen reader recipe uses.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger" id="resources">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Resources</p>
         <h2>Read-only resource URIs</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>a11ied://targets</code><span>The supported target list.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>a11ied://wcag/criteria/2.2</code><span>The criteria index for a version.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>a11ied://wcag/levels/2.2</code><span>Level to criteria lists for a version.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>a11ied://wcag/coverage/2.2</code><span>Coverage data for every criterion in a version.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
         The three versioned resources are registered for both <code>2.1</code> and
         <code>2.2</code>, which makes seven resources in total. All return
         <code>application/json</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="effects">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Side effects</p>
         <h2>What each tool touches</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         <code>wcag_show</code>, <code>wcag_criteria</code>, <code>wcag_search</code>, and
         <code>wcag_rule</code> read local files (<code>wcag_rule</code> also reads the installed
         axe-core package for help text). <code>run_axe</code>, <code>tree</code>, and
         <code>audit</code> open the target in browser automation. <code>doctor</code> probes the host
         and runs a short recording check on macOS.
      </p>
      <p>
         The <code>sr_*</code> tools change the state of the machine. A real session puts VoiceOver
         or NVDA under automation control until the client calls <code>sr_session</code> with
         <code>action: "stop"</code>.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
